b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Kammerer</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1050154-the-nature-of-civilization?p=1050362#post1050362\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">An example of course. Who still believes in slavery, especially as justified by Aristotle?</div> </div> </div> </div>Whew. Thank god. The wording was ambiguous so I got very worried. <br /> <br /> I'll just stow my ban hammer and move along. Have a nice day.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/OnyxGraphics/OnyxMod.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Onyx Path Moderator </span><br /> <b>Mod Voice is <u><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">RED</span></u>*</b><br /> <i>*All other colors should be read aloud in the voice of Don Knotts</i><b>.</b> <hr /><b><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/18519-pendragon-s-homebrew-hub\" target=\"_blank\">Hacks and House Rules Hub Thread</a></b><br /> <br /> <b>CofD Hacks By Splat: <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/13vBLaS5Ua1Ez-cVyK6uFa4ssvYeIIZpoA5GQTXytfcA/edit\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#B22222\">Vampire</span></a> | <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1sqltG7X-C_MdEe00PtVOZmki8V_K1M1FpQtLwTvb2jg/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#006400\">Werewolf</span></a> | <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1bpr1EjQ4RfZ5dz-tXmJJ93P2f_l7fUIUwpPLWs9Z-SI/edit#\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800080\">Mage</span></a> | <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1vKpSTktCUuUaqxXncG4txQPtvPclzXlFZEEX7tbrTSg/edit#\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Mummy</span></a> | <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1Spynbcs_VgCDHdooKWXobZNe6C9CMjONeRTlvXIq9fI/edit?usp=drive_web\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Demon</span></a></b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/148-pendragon","name":"PenDragon","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar148_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":6,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1657"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-17T03:01:16-08:00","id":1050394,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>PenDragon</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1050154-the-nature-of-civilization?p=1050368#post1050368\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Whew. Thank god. The wording was ambiguous so I got very worried. <br /> <br /> I'll just stow my ban hammer and move along. Have a nice day.</div> </div> </div> </div>That one was pretty much obvious. Believing that humans are inherently good... Meanwhile many people clearly believe in terrible things like those he described and many others.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12880-karlgust","name":"Karlgust","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"614"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-17T03:15:26-08:00","id":1050398,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aya Tari</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1050154-the-nature-of-civilization?p=1050285#post1050285\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Well, considering that the question concerning goodness and human rights, I would say that it is a moral question, so philosophy would be an appropriate to the discussion. Since you are presenting arguments against the questions, rather than answering the questions, I will assume that you do not have anything meaningful to contribute to the discussion and are just trying to be argumentative for the sake of arguing. If you want to contribute to the discussion, feel free to answer the question or to discuss the answers of other people. Otherwise, please stop trolling the discussion.</div> </div> </div> </div>You can use philosophy to argue about the definitions of good or bad, and about moralities.<br /> <br /> But, when we talk about behaviorism, philosophy have ZERO authority over it.<br /> <br /> You'll need to use several sciences for that<br /> -Psicology<br /> -Sociology<br /> -Evolution<br /> -Archaeology<br /> -Animal Behaviorism<br /> <br /> <br /> I don't care what a bunch of philosophers have to say about human behavior, I don't care if Plato or Socrats or Kant said that we are &quot;inherently good&quot; or &quot;inherently evil&quot;, they don't have any scientific base to be able to make such assumptions, because none of those philosophers EVER made any study of human behavior using the scientific methody. So, their <b>personal opinions</b> (because that's, in fact, what it is), have no bigger influence on the subject than saying that something is made of fire, air, water and dirty.<br /> <br /> So, philosophy will help giving the classification of morals, but won't help understanding how do humans behavior operate under said classification<br /> <br /> Ie: philosophy is the mathematical language, but not the Calculus itself. Therefore, you can't use it to give you the results, alone","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12880-karlgust","name":"Karlgust","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"614"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-17T06:21:21-08:00","id":1050456,"title":"","content":"I post this because it seems to be on topic (it's the first of a list, and it doesn't necessarily represents my toughts on the matter, albeit I foud it of interest):<br /> <br /> <div class=\"videocontainer with-aspect-ratio videocontainer-horizontal\" style=\"aspect-ratio: 16/9;\"> <a class=\"video-frame\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bjnvtRgpg6g&amp;index=1&amp;list=PL22J3VaeABQAGbKJ NDrRa6GNL0iL4KoOj\" data-code=\"bjnvtRgpg6g\" data-provider=\"youtube\" data-height=\"0\" data-width=\"0\" itemprop=\"video\"> <div class=\"videothumbnail\" style=\"\"> <img src=\"https://img.youtube.com/vi/bjnvtRgpg6g/hqdefault.jpg\" alt=\"\" /> <div class=\"videothumbnailoverlay\"></div> </div> </a> </div>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-17T07:35:20-08:00","id":1050484,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aya Tari</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1050154-the-nature-of-civilization#post1050154\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">1. Is human nature inherently good (or, at least, inherently innocent)? If so, how do we know?</div> </div> </div> </div>This is a very separate question from your others and I won't touch it with a ten-foot pole. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aya Tari</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1050154-the-nature-of-civilization#post1050154\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">2. Are human civilizations innately good? If so, how can the despots, genocide, slavery, and war across five thousand years of human history be explained?</div> </div> </div> </div>Civilization is simply a higher order of complexity of human society. As such it allows for greater good and greater evil than the lack of civilization. It is simply an amplifier of human nature, and doesn't change it.<br /> <br /> You could argue that civilization makes it more likely that a given person's basic needs of food, shelter, security, etc. are met and therefore gives them more opportunity to engage higher thought, and you could argue that higher thought allows for more &quot;good&quot;...but I wouldn't.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aya Tari</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1050154-the-nature-of-civilization#post1050154\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">3. Does civilization need to be chained to a focus on human rights (including the reproductive freedoms of women) to prevent the rise of despots, genocide, slavery, and war? If not, how do stop such things from occurring?</div> </div> </div> </div>The reproductive freedoms of women are just one example of an institutional environment making it more or less likely that a given person can choose their future. A woman in a patriarchal society without reproductive freedom is by far most likely to end up married, bearing children, and focused on raising them. A woman in a less patriarchal society who can choose whether to reproduce or not is much more able to choose a different future. Similarly, a black man born into a slave family in Georgia in 1810 has far less choice about his future state than if he were born in Minnesota in 1990. it's just a way the deck is stacked.<br /> <br /> So yes, addressing issues like these allows people more choice about their future, which is pretty close to an unalloyed &quot;good&quot;. In a truly egalitarian society where everyone has a fair shot, perhaps there's less chance of despots/slavery/all the things you listed. That said, it's not as easy to remove these narrowed paths. For example, being born into a rich family allows more choice than being born into a poor family. So should we communally share all property and equalize all economic states? It's much less clear whether that would reduce the likelihood of despots/slavery/etc.<br /> <br /> Finally - your asking these questions implies a fourth question: &quot;Has the Order of Reason / Technocracy's work to build a more complex civilization caused more 'good' or 'evil'? Is it 'good' to civilize humanity?&quot; I would argue this question isn't really related to the other three. It doesn't matter empirically whether civilization produces good - what matters is whether the OoR/Tech's approach has produced good. In other words - who cares whether it's possible to fuck up civilization - <i>did</i> the Technocracy fuck it up in reality? The obvious answer is <i>yes</i>, because the game setting is the World of <i>Darkness</i>.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7356-haldir2012","name":"haldir2012","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"148"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-17T09:15:56-08:00","id":1050535,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aya Tari</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1050154-the-nature-of-civilization#post1050154\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">1. Is human nature inherently good (or, at least, inherently innocent)? If so, how do we know?<br /> <br /> 2. Are human civilizations innately good? If so, how can the despots, genocide, slavery, and war across five thousand years of human history be explained?</div> </div> </div> </div> The terms good/innocent need more explanation here. As the first response indicated, there is no objective criteria to be had. You'd probably get further if the question was if human nature/civilization are inherently destructive/self-destructive.<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> 3. Does civilization need to be chained to a focus on human rights (including the reproductive freedoms of women) to prevent the rise of despots, genocide, slavery, and war? If not, how do stop such things from occurring? </div> </div> </div>The term 'chained to a focus on human rights' is extremely vague. What exactly is to be considered chained to such a cause? Just a lot of laws protecting them or a strong cultural focus?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ajax</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1050154-the-nature-of-civilization?p=1050160#post1050160\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">What do those questions have to do with the Technocratic Union (assuming something like an amalgamated objective position is probably) or the many, many, MANY different sub-groups glommed together in the Nine Traditions (where it is pretty much 100% certain there is NO such position)?<br /> <br /> What underlying assumptions are these three questions even based on?</div> </div> </div> </div> I assume (just guessing) they try to illuminate that if human nature/civilization is inherently destructive/self-destructive, it would be better to have something like the Technocracy protecting humanity from itself.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12552-monalfie","name":"Monalfie","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12552_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1261"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-17T10:14:16-08:00","id":1050566,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aya Tari</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1050154-the-nature-of-civilization?p=1050233#post1050233\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Scientific backup? How about the entire archeological record before 5,000 BC where there is no evidence of large-scale conflicts between groups of human beings? How about the entire historical record of human civilization since 3,000 BC, which is a litany of despots, genocide, slavery, and war that kept only getting worse until World War II shocked some sense into people and caused them to start to wonder what the purpose of human civilization was?<br /> <br /> I am not advocating a return to a primitive society. I appreciate simple things like air conditioning and toilet paper. However, I do not think that we need to tolerate despots, genocide, slavery, or war in exchange for getting to benefit from air conditioning and toilet paper.</div> </div> </div> </div>I feel obligated to point out that your entire premise seems to be that because despots, genocide, slavery and war are associated with civilization, that there's a causal relationship between them. You haven't really offered any evidence in support of that notion, making your entire argument undone by <i>post hoc ergo propter hoc</i> thinking. <br /> <br /> And that's even if we accept your premise as true, that before we went in an agricultural direction, we didn't have genocide and war to worry about. That's just not true. For genocide, let's talk about the Neanderthal. It's by no means settled how that species went extinct, but one of the many viable hypotheses is that Neanderthal extinction was either caused by or by violent conflict with Homo sapiens, or that violence against Neanderthals played a contributing role to their extinction.<br /> <br /> The notion that hunters/gatherers are peaceful is not one supported by the data. Conflict and intra-tribal warfare are ubiquitous features of hunter-gatherer societies, including conflicts over limited resources, such as prey and water according to the <a href=\"http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0047248406002193\" target=\"_blank\">Journal of Human Evolution</a>. And even though there's no evidence for this, I consider it probable that sexual abuse and abduction and rape of women that would result from such conflicts is easily analogous to slavery.<br /> <br /> In summary, your argument rests on a logical fallacy, but even if it didn't, your premises are flawed and demonstrably untrue.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I seem to have acquired a site for running play by post games. This is unexpected and frightening and come watch either the glorious play or the magnificent train wreck:<br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://madnessforums.com/forums/index.php\" target=\"_blank\">The Malkavian Madness Network</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1515-benjcano","name":"BenjCano","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1515_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1276"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-17T10:41:46-08:00","id":1050581,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aya Tari</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1050154-the-nature-of-civilization#post1050154\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">1. Is human nature inherently good (or, at least, inherently innocent)? If so, how do we know?</div> </div> </div> </div>It's pretty hard to prove inherent goodness. I'd say humanity is flawed, but in the broad strokes history is a general trend towards moral societies (with a lot of stumbles) and new moral principles arise from already accepted ones. We don't fully understand what we are striving for and we don't always achieve it, but we try.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> 2. Are human civilizations innately good? If so, how can the despots, genocide, slavery, and war across five thousand years of human history be explained? </div> </div> </div>See my answer to (1).<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> 3. Does civilization need to be chained to a focus on human rights (including the reproductive freedoms of women) to prevent the rise of despots, genocide, slavery, and war? If not, how do stop such things from occurring? </div> </div> </div>Just going to pause for a second to take in how you used used chain imagery in a question about human rights.<br /> <br /> And before anyone thinks I'm taking a cheap shot, it's actually fitting. wars have been fought and people conquered in the name of freedom and quality of life. The British Empire, among its many reasons for colonialism, held uplifting the rest of humanity as a duty. Tyranny so often starts with the best of intentions, and sometimes even continues with them.<br /> <br /> There is no motivation so noble that it cannot turn into oppression. Put one on an altar and it will likely happen faster. It takes vigilance and self-reflection and an awareness that we could already be doing terrible wrongs. <br /> <br /> That's one of the most important things about the Technocracy. If they've fallen from their lofty ideals they are tyrants, but if they never fell they'd still be tyrants. <br /> <br /> However, people have picked up the reigns of power and put them back down. It has been done before. If the Technocracy went forward with their eyes open, fully conscious that they themselves were the greatest threat to the world and scrutinizing every one of their actions, they could actually be something noble.<br /> <br /> As hero of the revolution Ben Parker once said, With Absolute Power Comes Absolute Responsibility. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage: The Ice-ension: An Epic Game of Reality on the Rink","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","name":"Ramnesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-17T11:28:36-08:00","id":1050611,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ramnesis</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">And before anyone thinks I'm taking a cheap shot, it's actually fitting. wars have been fought and people conquered in the name of freedom and quality of life. The British Empire, among its many reasons for colonialism, held uplifting the rest of humanity as a duty. Tyranny so often starts with the best of intentions, and sometimes even continues with them.<br /> <br /> There is no motivation so noble that it cannot turn into oppression. Put one on an altar and it will likely happen faster. It takes vigilance and self-reflection and an awareness that we could already be doing terrible wrongs. </div> </div> </div> </div>Quite so. This it's why I find this subject to be so difficult. The very nature of &quot;goodness&quot; can be put into question. When you need to *force* someone to *act right* it's clear that *goodness* can be opposed. But how can be evil to chain ourselves to good? . Both major and minor exploitations of people have been, and still are, justified as to archieve good or to defeat evil. There's an idea of what's good or moraly desirable that is causing dissonance, and that's why I hesitate in placing *the good* as the sole Idea that's guardian of our fate. <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-17T11:33:52-08:00","id":1050616,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ramnesis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1050154-the-nature-of-civilization?p=1050581#post1050581\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Just going to pause for a second to take in how you used used chain imagery in a question about human rights.<br /> <br /> And before anyone thinks I'm taking a cheap shot, it's actually fitting. wars have been fought and people conquered in the name of freedom and quality of life. The British Empire, among its many reasons for colonialism, held uplifting the rest of humanity as a duty. Tyranny so often starts with the best of intentions, and sometimes even continues with them.<br /> <br /> There is no motivation so noble that it cannot turn into oppression. Put one on an altar and it will likely happen faster. It takes vigilance and self-reflection and an awareness that we could already be doing terrible wrongs. <br /> <br /> That's one of the most important things about the Technocracy. If they've fallen from their lofty ideals they are tyrants, but if they never fell they'd still be tyrants. <br /> <br /> However, people have picked up the reigns of power and put them back down. It has been done before. If the Technocracy went forward with their eyes open, fully conscious that they themselves were the greatest threat to the world and scrutinizing every one of their actions, they could actually be something noble.<br /> <br /> As hero of the revolution Ben Parker once said, With Absolute Power Comes Absolute Responsibility. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /></div> </div> </div> </div>Well, fascism sought out the perfect society, and nazism the perfect men; and socialism strived for the equality of all men. The result of all those &quot;utopias&quot; were the most barbaric stain on human history, and the worst nightmares imaginable (and communism/socialism were 10 times worse than nazi/facism). The revolutionary France fought for <i>Libert\u00e9, Egalit\u00e9, Fraternit\u00e9</i>, and to achieve those Noble goals, they used the guilhotine to behead hundreds of thousands under the regime that received the fitting name of <b>Terror</b>.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12880-karlgust","name":"Karlgust","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"614"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-17T11:40:12-08:00","id":1050619,"title":"","content":"Imposed or forced &quot;good&quot; is how you end up with the thought police and the gulags for those &quot;evil people&quot;, be they evil jews, or greedy capitalists, or whatever kind of person is the &quot;hateful&quot; group (funny how this same speech that condemns &quot;evil people with unfair privileges&quot; is beeing used for white males... And yet, people don't realize it's the same thing the nazis did to jews)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12880-karlgust","name":"Karlgust","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"614"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-17T11:42:22-08:00","id":1050622,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aya Tari</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1050154-the-nature-of-civilization?p=1050233#post1050233\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> <br /> Scientific backup? How about the entire archeological record before 5,000 BC where there is no evidence of large-scale conflicts between groups of human beings? How about the entire historical record of human civilization since 3,000 BC, which is a litany of despots, genocide, slavery, and war that kept only getting worse until World War II shocked some sense into people and caused them to start to wonder what the purpose of human civilization was?<br /> <br /></div> </div> </div> </div>Incorrect. There is definitely evidence of conflict as massive as was possible considering the populations as determined by subsistence (rorrager... that's what professionals call it now, not &quot;hunter/gatherer&quot;) and carrying capacity of the environment. And of genocide. Way before 5000BC. Slavery is harder to detect considering the technology, but that is an interesting question and definitely NOT one to which one can give a conclusive answer.<br /> <br /> There are trade-offs between past lifeways and current lifeways. Indeed, modern Western culture has many distinctive maladaptive qualities (like forming family group organizations only found in the absolute most resource poor populations... indicating that, though they have access to more stuff, they are actually barely surviving...). Our diet is abyssmal, but it's genetically programmed. We finally have nigh unlimited access to all the previously rare foodstuffs that our bodies are designed to crave, but too much of a good thing is a very bad thing indeed. But, in any case, if you want to compare past to present, present is going to wins hands down for reasons like &quot;penicillin&quot;.<br /> <br /> From the get go, these questions are riddled with assumptions and unstated terms that makes it almost impossible to answer unless we know what you're basing your arguments on. What does your use of the word &quot;civilization&quot; even mean?<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1062-ajax","name":"Ajax","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1062_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2319"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-17T11:45:19-08:00","id":1050624,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>BenjCano</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1050154-the-nature-of-civilization?p=1050566#post1050566\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The notion that hunters/gatherers are peaceful is not one supported by the data. Conflict and intra-tribal warfare are ubiquitous features of hunter-gatherer societies, including conflicts over limited resources, such as prey and water according to the <a href=\"http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0047248406002193\" target=\"_blank\">Journal of Human Evolution</a>.</div> </div> </div> </div>Quite. Let me one-up that: <a href=\"http://www.ibtimes.co.uk/hunter-gatherer-massacre-first-record-human-murder-shows-brutal-slaughter-men-women-children-1538990\" target=\"_blank\">http://www.ibtimes.co.uk/hunter-gath...ildren-1538990</a><br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The fossilised remains of 27 brutally murdered hunter-gatherers have been uncovered, showing the earliest human massacre ever recorded. The remains of six children and at least eight women, one of which was heavily pregnant, were discovered at the site of the murder in Kenya.<br /> <br /> &lt;...&gt;<br /> <br /> Miraz\u00f3n Lahr said: &quot;The Nataruk massacre may have resulted from an attempt to seize resources \u2013 territory, women, children, food stored in pots \u2013 whose value was similar to those of later food-producing agricultural societies, among whom violent attacks on settlements became part of life.&quot;<br /> <br /> The location of the Nataruk site was found in 2012. Dating techniques, including radiocarbon, used on the excavated material suggested the time of extermination could have been anywhere between 10,500 to 9,500 years ago. </div> </div> </div>But let's not let that get in the way of the 'Humans were so much better before civilization' fantasy.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://is.gd/wodchart\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Dice Probability Chart</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodcalc\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Dice Statistics Calculator</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodarmor\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Alternative Armor System</a><br /> <a href=\"https://is.gd/woddamage\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Alternative Damage Roll System</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/woddamage2\" target=\"_blank\">My explanation of cWoD Damage Levels</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodstrength\" target=\"_blank\">'Interesting' Strength Attribute Stuff</a><br /> <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodboom\" target=\"_blank\">EXPLOSIVE cWoD STUFF!</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodheresy\" target=\"_blank\">How Technocrats don't think they are Mages</a>","edited":{"userid":6606,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6606-ambrosia","user":"Ambrosia","date":".  Typo fixing! 02-17-2017, 05:27 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6606-ambrosia","name":"Ambrosia","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6606_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1567"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-17T13:26:38-08:00","id":1050686,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ambrosia</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1050154-the-nature-of-civilization?p=1050624#post1050624\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Qutie. Let me one-up that: <a href=\"http://www.ibtimes.co.uk/hunter-gatherer-massacre-first-record-human-murder-shows-brutal-slaughter-men-women-children-1538990\" target=\"_blank\">http://www.ibtimes.co.uk/hunter-gath...ildren-1538990</a><br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> But let's not let that get in the way of the 'Humans were so much better before civilization' fantasy.</div> </div> </div> </div>Never let crude facts get in the way of a good narrative. That's how religions and non-scientific philosophies survived for thousands of years.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12880-karlgust","name":"Karlgust","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"614"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-17T15:22:44-08:00","id":1050759,"title":"","content":"When faced with facts that disagree with our own beliefs, we have two options. Dismantle our beliefs or dismantle the facts.<br /> Since people tend to identify themselves by their beliefs, to dismantle the belief requires the dismantling of themselves as well.<br /> <br /> It's so much easier and far less painful to dismantle the facts instead.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/959615-chronicle-report-keepers-of-the-wyck\" target=\"_blank\">Keepers of the Wyck: A Chronicle I'm running</a> FINALE: Chapter 39: Green Fairy","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12193-saikou","name":"Saikou","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12193_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1406"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-17T16:14:04-08:00","id":1050782,"title":"","content":"Yes, there was interpersonal violence in the past, and the murder of 27 people out of the 6 million living at that time was horrific, but you are bringing up the exceptions that prove the rule (the vast majority of the tens of thousands of human remains lack evidence of interpersonal violence). For all we know, since the bodies were not buried, it could have been the dump site of an early serial killer (with the victims being dumped off over the course of months or years) because the exact date of each murder cannot be determined by carbon dating. In fact, considering the low population density of humans, it is much more likely that the victims were dropped off individually over time instead of in a small group, as you would have had to have combed 225 square miles to find a group that size on average. <br /> <br /> Now, compare that one horrific event (either early form of inter-group conflict or an early serial killer) and compare with the thousands of recorded genocides committed by civilization. When the global population was just 200 million people, Julius Caesar was responsible for slaughtering 150,000 members of a fleeing Germanic tribe (<a href=\"http://www.ibtimes.co.uk/roman-genocide-battlefield-where-julius-caesar-slaughtered-150000-tribespeople-discovered-1533067\" target=\"_blank\">http://www.ibtimes.co.uk/roman-genoc...overed-1533067</a>). Compare that event, which was just in a long list of Roman Genocides, to the early conflict/serial killer during the Neolithic, and it is hardly a comparison. The Neolithic conflict event killed 0.00045% of humanity over an indeterminate time period while the Roman Genocide killed 0.075% of humanity over three days. The Roman Genocide was over one hundred sixty times as bad (proportionally to the global population) than the Neolithic conflict, and it is hardly unique in the history of civilization.<br /> <br /> Of course, you can defend the despots, genocides, slavery, and wars caused by civilization all you want, you will hardly be unique because there are always people willing to defend and justify atrocities (usually with some subjective morality). I believe that human beings should expect more from their civilizations, and that we should overthrow despots, fight against genocide, execute the slavers and slave owners, and have a sufficiently robust military that war becomes unthinkably expensive for anyone who thinks to engage in war against us. Civilization is not civilized, but we can make it civilized by focusing on human rights and making sure that anyone who violates the rights of another human pays the price.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11683-aya-tari","name":"Aya Tari","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5986"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-17T16:21:58-08:00","id":1050786,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ambrosia</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1050154-the-nature-of-civilization?p=1050624#post1050624\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Quite. Let me one-up that: <a href=\"http://www.ibtimes.co.uk/hunter-gatherer-massacre-first-record-human-murder-shows-brutal-slaughter-men-women-children-1538990\" target=\"_blank\">http://www.ibtimes.co.uk/hunter-gath...ildren-1538990</a><br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> But let's not let that get in the way of the 'Humans were so much better before civilization' fantasy.</div> </div> </div> </div>Indeed, That is a very recent (as in published recently), very good example.<br /> <br /> You can also go even further back than c. 8000 years ago. Neanderthals, as a species/sub-species of humanity that are burying their dead in a grave full of flowers and taking care of a member of their band who was badly injured (blinded in one eye, missing part of his leg... which almost certainly had to be amputated), taking care an old man who couldn't chew his own food due to loss of teeth, nor acquire said food very well due to advanced arthritis (all &quot;good&quot; &quot;civilized&quot; thing, depending on how you are using those terms, and can't really be termed &quot;less than human&quot; by all those quasi-Kantian philosophical tests) are also responsible for mass murder followed by cannibalism, in one case of a entire extended family. If the empathy exists to take care of the injured and aged and care for the dead, then the empathy exists to know EXACTLY what was going on when they killed the men, women and children, hacked open their skulls to get at the brains and break the bones for the marrow before tossing them into a hole in back of a cave.... That's 49,000 years old. (There's another that's approximately 100,000 years old as well, but less well excavated and you can't do some of the tests, like, say DNA to determine the victims were members of a family).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1062-ajax","name":"Ajax","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1062_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2319"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-17T17:32:00-08:00","id":1050812,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aya Tari</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1050154-the-nature-of-civilization?p=1050782#post1050782\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Of course, you can defend the despots, genocides, slavery, and wars caused by civilization all you want, you will hardly be unique because there are always people willing to defend and justify atrocities (usually with some subjective morality). I believe that human beings should expect more from their civilizations, and that we should overthrow despots, fight against genocide, execute the slavers and slave owners, and have a sufficiently robust military that war becomes unthinkably expensive for anyone who thinks to engage in war against us. Civilization is not civilized, but we can make it civilized by focusing on human rights and making sure that anyone who violates the rights of another human pays the price.</div> </div> </div> </div>This is still a forum about Mage: the Ascension, right?<br /> <br /> Is death less bad/evil if it occurs due to lack of food, or exposure to a sudden cold snap? You're not going to see telling archaeological evidence of those deaths but it's reasonable to expect they were much higher in prehistory than they are today. <br /> <br /> If your argument is that death is bad and we should seek the level of civilization that minimizes death, your answer is not prehistory. If your argument is instead that murder is bad, well, civilization increases murder because it increases our power over the world, and murder is death due to someone's power. Simply making everyone too weak to enact their will on the world hardly seems a just solution. <br /> <br /> Finally, I'll just note that you're proposing a Pax Romana (a superpower with such overwhelming martial might that major war doesn't happen) while you decry a war crime committed by Caesar. How do you propose to ensure all actions taken by this incredibly potent force are just?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7356-haldir2012","name":"haldir2012","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"148"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-18T07:54:50-08:00","id":1050995,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saikou</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1050154-the-nature-of-civilization?p=1050759#post1050759\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">When faced with facts that disagree with our own beliefs, we have two options. Dismantle our beliefs or dismantle the facts.<br /> Since people tend to identify themselves by their beliefs, to dismantle the belief requires the dismantling of themselves as well.<br /> <br /> It's so much easier and far less painful to dismantle the facts instead.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's exactly how the religious, supersticious and magical thinking operates. It is so for any strong convictions too, and even big figures in sciences have suffered from this - Einstein for example never accepted Quantum theories. Perhaps that's just human nature","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12880-karlgust","name":"Karlgust","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"614"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-18T08:16:51-08:00","id":1051002,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aya Tari</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1050154-the-nature-of-civilization?p=1050782#post1050782\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Yes, there was interpersonal violence in the past, and the murder of 27 people out of the 6 million living at that time was horrific, but you are bringing up the exceptions that prove the rule (the vast majority of the tens of thousands of human remains lack evidence of interpersonal violence). For all we know, since the bodies were not buried, it could have been the dump site of an early serial killer (with the victims being dumped off over the course of months or years) because the exact date of each murder cannot be determined by carbon dating. In fact, considering the low population density of humans, it is much more likely that the victims were dropped off individually over time instead of in a small group, as you would have had to have combed 225 square miles to find a group that size on average. <br /> <br /> Now, compare that one horrific event (either early form of inter-group conflict or an early serial killer) and compare with the thousands of recorded genocides committed by civilization. When the global population was just 200 million people, Julius Caesar was responsible for slaughtering 150,000 members of a fleeing Germanic tribe (<a href=\"http://www.ibtimes.co.uk/roman-genocide-battlefield-where-julius-caesar-slaughtered-150000-tribespeople-discovered-1533067\" target=\"_blank\">http://www.ibtimes.co.uk/roman-genoc...overed-1533067</a>). Compare that event, which was just in a long list of Roman Genocides, to the early conflict/serial killer during the Neolithic, and it is hardly a comparison. The Neolithic conflict event killed 0.00045% of humanity over an indeterminate time period while the Roman Genocide killed 0.075% of humanity over three days. The Roman Genocide was over one hundred sixty times as bad (proportionally to the global population) than the Neolithic conflict, and it is hardly unique in the history of civilization.<br /> <br /> Of course, you can defend the despots, genocides, slavery, and wars caused by civilization all you want, you will hardly be unique because there are always people willing to defend and justify atrocities (usually with some subjective morality). I believe that human beings should expect more from their civilizations, and that we should overthrow despots, fight against genocide, execute the slavers and slave owners, and have a sufficiently robust military that war becomes unthinkably expensive for anyone who thinks to engage in war against us. Civilization is not civilized, but we can make it civilized by focusing on human rights and making sure that anyone who violates the rights of another human pays the price.</div> </div> </div> </div>Dude, you're nuts.<br /> <br /> Can you not see what you're doing? Your justifications just look like the nuts that believe we were made by the alien annunnakis or the bible fanatics that believe in the great flood.<br /> <br /> Your way of thinking is COMPLETELY dishonest. You're NOT putting things on a rational perspective. That's not the way the scientific thinking works. You have your truth, and you try to ajust the facts to it. You're rationalizing (oh, it probably were a serial killer, over a large period of time). That's absolutely an-scientific. You must make your theories according to the facts, not trying to adjust the facts to fit your theories.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12880-karlgust","name":"Karlgust","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"614"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-18T08:29:12-08:00","id":1051005,"title":"","content":"By the way, the Soviet Union, China, North Korea, Cuba, etc etc, they've executed millions based on that same premisse of &quot;executing the despots and slavers&quot; that you have Aya. That's exactly the shallow way of thinking that allowed for the worse and most opressive tyranies.<br /> <br /> What we must strive for is democracy, REAL individual freedoms and REAL freespeech (and not just freespeech for what we like to hear, but ABSOLUT freespeech, even to the things we find abhorent). The things you stand for, first of all are childish fantasies, and secondly, are EXACTLY the things that create the monstruous despots.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12880-karlgust","name":"Karlgust","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"614"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-18T11:23:14-08:00","id":1051075,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aya Tari</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1050154-the-nature-of-civilization?p=1050782#post1050782\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Yes, there was interpersonal violence in the past, and the murder of 27 people out of the 6 million living at that time was horrific, but you are bringing up the exceptions that prove the rule (the vast majority of the tens of thousands of human remains lack evidence of interpersonal violence). For all we know, since the bodies were not buried, it could have been the dump site of an early serial killer (with the victims being dumped off over the course of months or years) because the exact date of each murder cannot be determined by carbon dating. In fact, considering the low population density of humans, it is much more likely that the victims were dropped off individually over time instead of in a small group, as you would have had to have combed 225 square miles to find a group that size on average.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Wow. You open this thread with post hoc ergo propter hoc and bad premises, and now to try to salvage your premises, you resort to special pleading. You should know, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1048468-consensus-around-the-world?p=1048953#post1048953\">being a statistical analyst</a>, basic Bayesian reasoning. You should also know that if you must add an element to a claim in order to make that claim fit the evidence better, then the prior probability of the claim is reduced by the probability of that element being true. There is no logically valid way to avoid this.<br /> <br /> For example, if the evidence is unlikely on claim A, but becomes likely again if we add element X to A (so our claim now becomes X+A), and we have no reason to believe X is likely or unlikely, then the probability of X is 50% (or 0.50), and the prior probability of A is then reduced by 50%. In other words, P(A) becomes P(A) x P(X) = P(A) x 0.50. The prior probability is thus halved, just by claiming X.<br /> <br /> And the notion that the 27 victims in this site were the work of a serial killer isn't a claim that we have no reason to believe is likely or unlikely. We have very good data about the murder frequency of serial killers. According to a <a href=\"https://www.unodc.org/gsh/\" target=\"_blank\">global study on homicide,</a> a given murder victim is only .00039% likely to have been killed by a serial killer. That makes your original claim makes it way, way, WAY less probable than if you'd just left it alone.<br /> <br /> If you want to add MORE special pleadings to rescue your initial claim, then all their probabilities multiply to reduce the prior probability of the claim. If you want to say that serial killers were way more frequent or prolific back in prehistory, or that no one BUT serial killers did any murdering before civilization, we have no reason to believe that this brand new claim is either likely or unlikely. We can only give it a weight of 50%...but that means that your original claim is now only .000185% of its original probability.<br /> <br /> Given your logical fallacies and bad reasoning, I'm coming to the conclusion that you're either arguing in bad faith, or this notion you have on the basis of civilization and technology is one you hold as a piece of dogma that you will twist facts and evidence to support. Either way, I don't think that further conversation with you can be productive. Goodbye and good luck.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I seem to have acquired a site for running play by post games. This is unexpected and frightening and come watch either the glorious play or the magnificent train wreck:<br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://madnessforums.com/forums/index.php\" target=\"_blank\">The Malkavian Madness Network</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1515-benjcano","name":"BenjCano","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1515_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1276"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-18T12:41:48-08:00","id":1051107,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>BenjCano</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1050154-the-nature-of-civilization?p=1051075#post1051075\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">And the notion that the 27 victims in this site were the work of a serial killer isn't a claim that we have no reason to believe is likely or unlikely. We have very good data about the murder frequency of serial killers.</div> </div> </div> </div>Also, let's not forget that the concept of serial killers would not have worked back then in the first place.<br /> People did not live in pairs or small families. They did not wander alone off to work in some distant place or strange city. They lived in tribes. Single large groups. Not only would it be rare for a lone individual to wander *anywhere* for long enough to get murdered, it would be a magnitude more unlikely for a highly pregnant female.<br /> And those 27 people definitely belonged to the same group, because your friendly neighborhood tribe wasn't in short walking distance either. 27 different groups? Hell no.<br /> And, it most certainly wouldn't happen to 27 individual people from the same group, while Hoss at the bar goes 'Didya hear, them Ms. Miller's kid gone missin!'. Even a single missing person would be a thing that'd have ALL of them paying very close attention from then on.<br /> <br /> This is Kenya about 9000 years ago, not Ruralville, US. Those 27 people traveled and died together.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://is.gd/wodchart\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Dice Probability Chart</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodcalc\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Dice Statistics Calculator</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodarmor\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Alternative Armor System</a><br /> <a href=\"https://is.gd/woddamage\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Alternative Damage Roll System</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/woddamage2\" target=\"_blank\">My explanation of cWoD Damage Levels</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodstrength\" target=\"_blank\">'Interesting' Strength Attribute Stuff</a><br /> <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodboom\" target=\"_blank\">EXPLOSIVE cWoD STUFF!</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodheresy\" target=\"_blank\">How Technocrats don't think they are Mages</a>","edited":{"userid":6606,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6606-ambrosia","user":"Ambrosia","date":". 02-18-2017, 03:44 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6606-ambrosia","name":"Ambrosia","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6606_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1567"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-18T14:32:18-08:00","id":1051148,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>BenjCano</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1050154-the-nature-of-civilization?p=1051075#post1051075\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> Wow. You open this thread with post hoc ergo propter hoc and bad premises, and now to try to salvage your premises, you resort to special pleading. You should know, <a href=\"https://&amp;quot;&#91;url\" target=\"_blank\">http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1048468-consensus-around-the-world?p=1048953#post1048953&amp;quot;]being</a> a statistical analyst[/URL], basic Bayesian reasoning. You should also know that if you must add an element to a claim in order to make that claim fit the evidence better, then the prior probability of the claim is reduced by the probability of that element being true. There is no logically valid way to avoid this.<br /> <br /> For example, if the evidence is unlikely on claim A, but becomes likely again if we add element X to A (so our claim now becomes X+A), and we have no reason to believe X is likely or unlikely, then the probability of X is 50% (or 0.50), and the prior probability of A is then reduced by 50%. In other words, P(A) becomes P(A) x P(X) = P(A) x 0.50. The prior probability is thus halved, just by claiming X.<br /> <br /> And the notion that the 27 victims in this site were the work of a serial killer isn't a claim that we have no reason to believe is likely or unlikely. We have very good data about the murder frequency of serial killers. According to a <a href=\"https://&amp;quot;&#91;url\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.unodc.org/gsh/&amp;quot;]global</a> study on homicide,[/URL] a given murder victim is only .00039% likely to have been killed by a serial killer. That makes your original claim makes it way, way, WAY less probable than if you'd just left it alone.<br /> <br /> If you want to add MORE special pleadings to rescue your initial claim, then all their probabilities multiply to reduce the prior probability of the claim. If you want to say that serial killers were way more frequent or prolific back in prehistory, or that no one BUT serial killers did any murdering before civilization, we have no reason to believe that this brand new claim is either likely or unlikely. We can only give it a weight of 50%...but that means that your original claim is now only .000185% of its original probability.<br /> <br /> Given your logical fallacies and bad reasoning, I'm coming to the conclusion that you're either arguing in bad faith, or this notion you have on the basis of civilization and technology is one you hold as a piece of dogma that you will twist facts and evidence to support. Either way, I don't think that further conversation with you can be productive. Goodbye and good luck.</div> </div> </div> </div>Funny thing is, Aya will evoke the clausules of <i>Occan's Razor</i> when it suits his personal views, but forsake it's wisdom when it would get in the way of his convictions.<br /> <br /> That's the same kind of cognitive dissociation found in religious thinking","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12880-karlgust","name":"Karlgust","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"614"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-18T14:46:04-08:00","id":1051155,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ambrosia</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1050154-the-nature-of-civilization?p=1051107#post1051107\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Also, let's not forget that the concept of serial killers would not have worked back then in the first place.<br /> People did not live in pairs or small families. They did not wander alone off to work in some distant place or strange city. They lived in tribes. Single large groups. Not only would it be rare for a lone individual to wander *anywhere* for long enough to get murdered, it would be a magnitude more unlikely for a highly pregnant female.<br /> And those 27 people definitely belonged to the same group, because your friendly neighborhood tribe wasn't in short walking distance either. 27 different groups? Hell no.<br /> And, it most certainly wouldn't happen to 27 individual people from the same group, while Hoss at the bar goes 'Didya hear, them Ms. Miller's kid gone missin!'. Even a single missing person would be a thing that'd have ALL of them paying very close attention from then on.<br /> <br /> This is Kenya about 9000 years ago, not Ruralville, US. Those 27 people traveled and died together.</div> </div> </div> </div>That idea were absolutely preposterous, just a desperate attempt to counter argument the raw facts. Not only that, the serial killer himself COULDN'T live alone in some cave... There is a reason why humans live in groups, survival rates alone on the wilds having only sticks and rocks is minimum. So, a serial killer would still need to live in a group... But that would mean that his tribemen were ok with his &quot;peculiar&quot; needs...<br /> <br /> It's totally nonsense. Pregnant women wandering alone in the wilds? No no no, all those ideas are beyond absurd","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12880-karlgust","name":"Karlgust","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"614"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}